#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57fe64c-23e2-57d1-b7fc-3543c69342f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/id #uuid "767b530b-c488-5560-8850-a8f2168648e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5760" :question/problem "Let $q(x)=2x^3+3x^2-18x+8$. Given that $q(2) = 0$, factor $q(x)$ completely and identify all three of its roots." :question/worked-solution "If $q(2) = 0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x)$. To find the remaining zeros, we need to find the other factors of $q(x)$.\n\nSo $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x)$, we know that $(x-2)$ divides $q(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $q(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-18$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $14$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3+3x^2-18x+8}{x-2} = 2x^2+7x-4\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x^2+7x-4)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x-1)(x+4)\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to $0$ and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 4 &= 0 & 2x - 1|= 0 & x - 2|= 0 \\\\ x|=-4 & x|= \\frac{1}{2} & x|= 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "299354d9-f716-512e-b682-d82b97d03775" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/id #uuid "811e52fd-0786-5363-ae33-aeeb1378046b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5763" :question/problem "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)=x^3+2x^2-x-2$, factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "To find the remaining roots, we need to find the other factors of $f(x)$.\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$. So, to begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+2x^2-x-2}{x+2} = x^2-1\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x^2-1)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x+1)(x-1)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3c42bac-2253-5ceb-9b1f-45adfc8a61e9" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial With Only One Real Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "194dad70-c5fe-597c-a2a8-bcec1d699595" :question/math-academy-id "e-6205" :question/problem "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20$, factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $20$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-2$ | $12$ | $-20$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-6$ | $10$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20}{x+2} = x^2 - 6x + 10\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10)\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\nb^2 - 4ac = (-6)^2 - 4(1)(10) = -4 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3f33-1b17-5b5e-b196-2abab5504f97" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial That Factors Over the Reals But Not the Rationals"}] :question/id #uuid "e119ff72-d5a1-57da-90a1-60cc9d15ee87" :question/math-academy-id "e-6206" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2$, factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2}{x+1} = x^2 - 2x - 2\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1) (x^2 - 2x -2)\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = b^2 - 4ac = (-2)^2 - 4(1)(-2) = 12 > 0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1)(x^2 - 2x - 2)\n$$\n\n**Important Note**: Although the quadratic polynomial $(x^2 - 2x - 2)$ does not factor over the rational numbers, it *does* have real roots, because $\\mathcal D > 0$. Therefore, if we were asked to find all of the real roots of $f(x)$, we would need to solve the equations\n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x^2 - 2x - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nThe quadratic equation could be solved using the quadratic formula or by completing the square."}]}
